MPs attempts to stop no deal next week  depend on a small group of conservative  back benches if they rebel against their  government Boris Johnson could well be  defeated but the price of rebellion  could be there political careers in  Westminster the group which includes a  number of former ministers is known as  the gore quid squad so it's fitting that  we're joined by the former justice  secretary David gorg very good morning  to you look just to clarify in all of  this in terms of this suspension this  prorogation of parliament is there  anything illegal or unconstitutional  about it I think some of the language is  has been a little over the top and I  think this constitutes a coup but I do  think it's wrong I think we are entering  into a period which is crucial for this  country we have got big decisions to  make and the government should be  scrutinized by Parliament's who say  whether that's the preparations for no  deal or the attempts to reach a deal  Parliament should be scrutinizing what  the government is doing in Parliament  needs to be sitting to do that and in  fact I think Parliament should be  sitting more days over the next few  weeks than it would normally do not  fewer so I think it is wrong to have  suspended Parliament for that five-week  period I mean I know this is a matter in  front of the courts it may well be that  legally the government is entitled to do  so but I think as a matter of judgment  Parliament should be sitting throughout  September and October given the  particular circumstances that we're in  yet that wasn't going to be the case as  far as we understood it conference  season is under way I mean the message  from from number 10 is actually this is  only affecting four or five days of  Parliament sitting I think the  likelihood is that given a widespread  sense that Parliament should be sitting  during these crucial weeks in late  September and early October I think the  likelihood is that Parliament would have  sought to ensure that there were extra  days that Parliament was sitting during  the conference recess period and one of  the reasons why I think the government  has done that is to close that  possibility off and I do think the in  our system whereby government is held to  account where ministers are held to  account by Parliament that's what should  happen I was a minister for nine years I  know it can be frustrating to be held to  account by Parliament but that is part  of the job and that's what should be  happening over the course of the next  few weeks is there an argument to be  made it is being made and you will have  heard it many times that says in all of  this we've had this huge democratic  process of which Parliament agreed that  they would listen to the will of the  people nothing's happened in three years  you've not been able to agree with drol  agreement in the House of Commons if  Boris Johnson did not take this action  to you know ultimately hinder people  trying to stop as leaving with a No Deal  brexit the in fact we'd end up just  remaining we wouldn't be able to enforce  the will of the people well first of all  I regret the fact that Parliament did  not support the withdrawal agreement I  voted for it three times had my way  prevailed we would have left the  European Union what five months ago so  I'm not someone who is wanting to  frustrate the referendum result I want  us to leave the European Union but I  want us to do so with a deal because  without that as you know frankly our  departure will be very different from  the prospectus that was put to the  British people in the 2016 referendum  campaign where what was said is that we  will be able to leave in a smooth and  orderly way we would have a deal we  wouldn't have tariffs and quotas we'd be  able to trade with the European Union  just as we have done up until now a No  Deal brexit if we're looking at the  substance will be a very different  nature and it will have an impact on  people's jobs and livelihoods living  standards our security and I think the  integrity of the United Kingdom because  I think it will encourage for example  Northern Ireland to unite with the  Republic of Ireland and all courage  separatists in Scotland so this is a  very very bad outcome for the country  and I don't think it has a mandate from  the 2016 referendum and I don't think it  should happen unless Parliament consents  to it you could be seeing the Prime  Minister tomorrow well I think it's been  reported there are there is a meeting  that is trees  I will have an opportunity to speak to  the Prime Minister tomorrow and to say  what well I want to hear from him as to  what his his plan to deliver a deal when  are we putting forward proposals to deal  with this backstop issue which is the  the one issue he has identified as the  problem within the withdrawal agreement  I want to hear how he's going to address  that and I want to hear how he's plans  to deliver the legislation if we get a  deal by the 31st of October because I at  the moment frankly I can't see how he's  got time to do that this is where we end  up in a whole world of contradiction  though isn't it in the sense that you  say you want to see a deal you want us  you want to hear from the Prime Minister  tomorrow how he's going to achieve a  deal there is an argument to be made  that says if you then try to thwart a no  deal breaks in as many people are trying  to do in which he appears to be trying  to avoid then you actually stop him  getting a deal now I don't agree with  that I think it is worth looking what  are the things that are stopping us  getting a deal first of all everybody  agrees that there shouldn't be a hard  border between Northern Ireland and the  Republic of Ireland but given our plans  in terms of leaving customs union and so  on  and you know we need to find a practical  way to ensure that a hard border isn't  necessary now Boris Johnson doesn't like  the plans and withdrawal agreements on  that ass but he if he's got an  alternative then he should put that  forward and if he's got an alternative  that is workable I will fully support it  and I will vote for it but first of all  we need to have those plans second we  need to have a realistic plan to get the  legislation through the House of Commons  we've got to have the time and we've  also got to make sure that some of my  colleagues don't say well it's not just  the backstop it's something else and  we're still not gonna support it and I  think you know this idea that the threat  of no deal is going to make the European  Union member states move dramatically  I'm afraid I think is a bit of a fantasy  the European Union member states know  that no deal will have a bigger impact  on us than it will on them I think we  just have to face facts about that and  they are not going to move  their positions so that we end up with  needing a hard order between Northern  Ireland and the Republic okay so on the  basis that you're not gonna which is  unlikely that you're gonna hear anything  from the Prime Minister that's going to  completely satisfy you tomorrow what are  the plans for the coming week auu what  can you do to try to work and legislate  within Parliament to stop this No Deal  happening well I don't want to get into  too many details about us there's  obviously quite a lot of speculation  about it about seizing the order paper  and then taking legislation through my  view is that it is important that  Parliament is not excluded from this  process and that we shouldn't be it  shouldn't allow the government just to  sort of run the clock down and take us  out without a deal and I fear that that  is the direction that we're going in I  think it would be with respect you say  you don't want to get into too much  detail but in fact we it's the detail  that people need on this isn't it are  you going to try and in effect force  through an extension well I think the  detail will become very apparent in the  next few days but I think the important  thing is the Parliament should not be  excluded from this process as I say I  don't believe that  No Deal has a mandate from the 2016  referendum I don't think it's got the  support of Parliament but I think  Parliament does have to be still part of  this process and I don't think it should  be excluded and the problem is that if  we don't act in this week I think it's  likely that Parliament will be excluded  from this process firstly I would have  been happier to have left this for  another few weeks let's see how the  negotiations were progressing let's see  the government's plans on this but the  fact is that now that Parliament is  essentially only going to be sitting  this week and then by the time we come  back in in mid-october it will be too  late for Parliament to take any  effective action to prevent no deal so  the choice for Parliament seems to be  very clear if it's going to take action  it needs to take action in the next few  days when we talk about the the goal  quadsquad  as it were it seems like you you you  haven't got a clear-eyed  exactly want what you want to achieve in  the time that you do have available well  from my perspective I want us to leave  with a deal I think that is a better  outcome than either failing to deliver  the referendum result or leaving or what  action are you going to take to stop as  leaving without a date on October 31st  well that I think will become clearer as  we set out the legislation this week  assuming that we get the votes to to  take it if you get if you get out if you  get those votes or as you say but I mean  our effective the people will look at  this and say well the only real option  is to to force the prime minister to  apply for an extension well I think if  we are not ready to leave because we  haven't got a deal on the 31st of  October then we shouldn't leave on the  31st of October that's absolutely right  I think you know we we do have to  demonstrate or we've you know we may  well need to be flexible on this if we  can get a deal by the 31st of October  the prime minister comes back and he's  got legislation you can get through in  that time I will support it I'm not sure  that every Conservative MP can say that  but I will certainly support him if he  gets a if he gets a deal but if he  doesn't get a deal the responsible  course of action would be to extend  article 50 and seek to address these  issues and the foot in the time  afterwards so you are in that sense you  are pretty well aligned with with labour  and with what Kirsten has put forward  well I I believe that the right course  of action is to get a deal or and that's  where there is perhaps a difference with  me in the Labor Party and I've  consistently voted for a deal whereas  the Labour Party by and large has  opposed it but do I think the  responsible course of action is to leave  without a deal on the 31st of October  now I don't are you prepared to lose the  party whip on this because their reports  in the paper today that those  Conservative MPs who rebel could lose  the whip are you prepared to do that  well I've seen those reports I mean if  it is the position now that you know  defying a whip on a European vote is a  matter that you lose the  that the whip for the Conservative Party  then I think there's quite a lot of  conservative MPs who over the recent  months would have lost the the whip but  you know sometimes there is a point  where it is you have to judge between  your own personal interests and the  national interest and the national  interests has to come first but I hope  it doesn't come to that and I hope that  cooler and calmer heads will look at  this and think that trying to split the  Conservative Party in this way is not a  sensible way forward for the  Conservative Party or indeed for the  country one of your colleagues Oliver  latwon has said you know in effect  time isn't the issue here when it comes  to sorting this out it's getting a  majority that would them about this if  that doesn't happen and you don't get  this true where you would you then look  at supporting a vote of no confidence in  the government if that is brought  forward by the Labour Party well the  position I've consistently said is that  you know the country faces two big risks  a crash out on the 31st of October or a  Jeremy Corbyn government I don't want to  do anything that would facilitate a  Jeremy Corbyn government indeed one of  my worries about a No Deal brexit is  that it will create the chaos in which  Jeremy Corbyn could win a general  election so I I don't want him to become  Prime Minister but isn't that part of  the argument that some people would say  well you you say you want to do  everything to stop a No Deal brexit but  you're not prepared to do that  to support him getting into number 10  even if just for a very short period of  time I don't believe that Jeremy Corbyn  is fit to be Prime Minister and I don't  want to facilitate that okay David go up  very good to talk to you this morning  thank you very much indeed
 

